%erg 0.1
Title: R10 detect exfiltration through local relays
Created: 2026-09-04
Author: codex

--- log ---
2026-09-04T10:02Z codex created

--- body ---

## Context

Child of master ticket 0660. The syscall classifier ignores non-resolver
loopback connections and Unix-domain sockets. That is correct for ordinary
in-machine cooperation but creates a custody false green: a target may send
library text or a query to an already-running local relay which forwards it
off-machine outside the traced process tree. This child owns the end-to-end
relay detector, not general lifecycle coverage (0662).

## Actions

- Build harness-owned TCP-loopback and Unix-socket relays whose forwarding
  attempts are observable independently of the target process trace.
- Use distinctive synthetic library and query payload markers; never use the
  author's library content.
- Correlate receipt and attempted forwarding so ordinary local IPC remains
  allowed while data handed to a forwarding boundary is red.
- State the residual boundary for relays the harness cannot observe; do not
  describe direct-connect coverage as end-to-end coverage.

## Test

- [ ] Direct remote, hostname, TCP-relay and Unix-relay controls each fail
      through their intended detector.
- [ ] Benign loopback IPC that neither receives R10 data nor forwards stays
      green.
- [ ] A relay that never starts, never receives the marker, or never attempts
      forwarding makes its arm `not-run` rather than PASS.
- [ ] The control relay is outside the target's process tree, matching the hole.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Both local relay classes are covered with one-purpose controls.
- [ ] Artifacts distinguish direct attempts, relay receipt and relay forwarding.
- [ ] Residual end-to-end limits are disclosed beside the verdict.
- [ ] `make check` and `./tickets/erg check tickets/` pass.
